= String theory
{wiki}

String theory replaces point particles by one-dimensional objects whose histories are two-dimensional worldsheets.

= Compactification in string theory
{parent=String theory}

Compactification makes some target spatial dimensions compact and sufficiently small that lower-energy observations see an effective lower-dimensional spacetime. A simple geometry is a product of noncompact spacetime with a compact internal manifold; its <momentum> and winding sectors influence the effective spectrum. Reaching a four-dimensional model from a critical ten-dimensional superstring requires such additional geometric and field-theoretic data. <Worldsheet supersymmetry> alone does not choose the compactification or establish a realistic particle spectrum.

= Spinning string
{parent=String theory}

= Ramond–Neveu–Schwarz string
{c}
{synonym}

The spinning string adds <worldsheet Majorana fermions> and local <worldsheet supersymmetry> to the embedding coordinates. Its gauge-fixed quantum description is the Ramond–Neveu–Schwarz formulation of the superstring.

= GSO projection
{c}
{parent=Spinning string}
{title2=$P_{\mathrm{GSO}}$}
{wiki}

The Gliozzi–Scherk–Olive projection selects consistent worldsheet fermion-number and chirality sectors in an RNS string theory. In suitable supersymmetric choices it removes the tachyonic <Neveu–Schwarz sector> ground state and keeps a chosen <Ramond sector> chirality. Together with the consistent sector combinations it produces spacetime <fermions> and, for theories such as <Type II superstring theory>, a tachyon-free spectrum. The mere presence of classical <worldsheet supersymmetry> does not specify this projection or guarantee tachyon removal.

= Neveu–Schwarz sector
{c}
{parent=Spinning string}

Antiperiodic <worldsheet Majorana fermions> have half-integer modes in this sector.

= Ramond sector
{c}
{parent=Spinning string}

Periodic <worldsheet Majorana fermions> have integer modes and fermionic zero modes in this sector.

= Worldsheet supersymmetry
{parent=Spinning string}

Local <supersymmetry> on a <string worldsheet> relates embedding coordinates to their <worldsheet Majorana fermion> partners. Fixing this fermionic gauge symmetry introduces commuting <superconformal ghosts>.

= Worldsheet gravitino
{parent=Worldsheet supersymmetry}
{title2=$\chi_\mu$}

The worldsheet gravitino is the fermionic vector-spinor gauge field for local <worldsheet supersymmetry>, paired with the worldsheet metric in two-dimensional <supergravity>. In a locally supersymmetric string action it couples to the worldsheet supercurrent. Fixing its gauge and the bosonic <conformal gauge> leaves the usual flat rigid transformations on embeddings and <worldsheet Majorana fermions>; it should not be silently omitted when claiming an arbitrary curved-worldsheet locally supersymmetric action.

= Rigid worldsheet supersymmetry boundary term
{parent=Worldsheet supersymmetry}
{title2=$\delta\mathcal L=\partial_\mu J^\mu$}

In flat <conformal gauge>, take $\mathcal L=\partial X\cdot\partial X+i\alpha'\bar\psi\gamma^\mu\partial_\mu\psi$ and rigid transformations $\delta X=iA\bar\epsilon\psi$, $\delta\psi=B\gamma^\nu\partial_\nu X\epsilon$, with $A=\alpha'B$. The <Majorana Grassmann bilinear interchange> and <Clifford algebra> combine the variations into
$$
\delta\mathcal L=\partial_\mu\bigl(iA\partial_\nu X\cdot\bar\epsilon\gamma^\mu\gamma^\nu\psi\bigr).
$$
The identity is off shell. The integrated action is invariant when its boundary flux vanishes, for example on a closed worldsheet or with compatible supersymmetric endpoint conditions. Constant spinors and ordinary derivatives here refer to flat gauge; arbitrary curved worldsheets require covariant spinor derivatives and the locally supersymmetric completion.

= Superconformal ghost
{parent=Worldsheet supersymmetry}
{title2=$(\beta,\gamma)$}

The commuting <beta-gamma system> produced by <worldsheet supersymmetry> gauge fixing has <conformal weights> $(3/2,-1/2)$ and <central charge> $11$ per chiral sector.

= Worldsheet Majorana fermion
{parent=Worldsheet supersymmetry}
{title2=$\psi^m$}

Each real chiral fermion has <conformal weight> $1/2$ and contributes $1/2$ to the <central charge>. The <Ramond sector> and <Neveu–Schwarz sector> differ by periodic versus antiperiodic boundary conditions.

= String coupling
{title2=$g_s$}
{parent=String theory}
{wiki}

The string coupling controls topology change and string splitting and joining. A constant background <dilaton> $\Phi_0$ sets $g_s=e^{\Phi_0}$.

= String genus expansion
{parent=String coupling}

For a connected oriented closed-string worldsheet of genus $g$ with $n$ external closed-string vertices, the perturbative amplitude carries $g_s^{2g-2+n}$. The exponent combines the dilaton factor $g_s^{-\chi}$ for Euler characteristic $\chi=2-2g$ with one $g_s$ from each normalized vertex.

= String perturbation theory
{parent=String coupling}

String perturbation theory expands amplitudes in powers of the <String coupling> by summing over worldsheet topologies. It is reliable where the local effective coupling $e^{\Phi(X)}$ is small.

= Dilaton
{title2=$\Phi$}
{parent=String coupling}
{wiki}

The dilaton is a scalar closed-string background field coupled to the worldsheet Ricci scalar. Its constant mode determines the <string coupling>, while spacetime variation modifies the background-field equations.

= Lorentzian dilaton coupling sign convention
{c}
{parent=Dilaton}

With worldsheet signature $(-,+)$, $e^{iS_L}=e^{-S_E}$, and the curvature convention $R[e^{2\omega}h]=e^{-2\omega}(R[h]-2\Box_h\omega)$, the usual negative Lorentzian kinetic action becomes positive in Euclidean signature. A positive Lorentzian term $(4\pi)^{-1}\int\sqrt{-h}\,R\Phi$ becomes a negative Euclidean curvature coupling. Therefore the conventional Euclidean <dilaton> with positive curvature coupling is $\varphi=-\Phi$. The <leading metric-dilaton Weyl condition> is $R_{ab}+2\nabla_a\nabla_b\varphi=0$, or $R_{ab}-2\nabla_a\nabla_b\Phi=0$ with that literal positive Lorentzian coupling. Keeping the same named field and reversing the action's curvature-coupling sign reverses the Hessian term. The continuation follows directly from $d\tau=-i\,d\tau_E$ and $S_E=-iS_L$; the geometric scalar curvature continues without an extra arbitrary sign.

= Bosonic string theory
{parent=String theory}
{wiki}

Bosonic string theory quantizes a relativistic string using only bosonic worldsheet fields. Lorentz-invariant quantization requires 26-dimensional target spacetime and contains a tachyonic ground state.

= Open string
{parent=Bosonic string theory}
{wiki}

An open string has a worldsheet with boundary. Its endpoint conditions may be <Neumann boundary conditions>, <Dirichlet boundary conditions>, or mixtures selected independently in target-space directions.

= Open-string mode expansion
{parent=Open string}

In <conformal gauge>, a free open-string coordinate with <Neumann boundary conditions> has a cosine expansion. Canonical quantization turns its nonzero Fourier coefficients into <string oscillators>.

= Neumann-Dirichlet open-string boundary condition
{c}
{parent=Open-string mode expansion}
{title2=$\partial_\sigma X(0)=0,\quad X(\pi)=y$}

= ND boundary condition
{c}
{synonym}

A transverse <open string> coordinate has an ND boundary condition when one endpoint obeys a <Neumann boundary condition> and the other a <Dirichlet boundary condition>. On a strip of width $\pi$, its nonconstant spatial <eigenfunctions> are $\cos((n+1/2)\sigma)$ when the free endpoint is at zero. They solve the endpoint equations because their derivatives vanish at zero and their values vanish at $\pi$. Reversing the endpoints gives sine <eigenfunctions> with the same frequencies. The fixed endpoint removes the dynamical <worldsheet zero mode>.

= Bosonic ND boundary-changing conformal weight
{parent=Neumann-Dirichlet open-string boundary condition}
{title2=$h_{\mathrm{ND}}=1/16$}

= ND twist conformal weight
{c}
{synonym}

For one free boson, the mixed ND open-string sector has strip vacuum energy $E_0=1/48$. The strip-to-plane transformation relates this energy to the plane <Virasoro algebra> zero mode by $E_0=h-c/24$, with <central charge> $c=1$. Hence its ground <conformal weight> is $h=1/48+1/24=1/16$. For $d$ independent ND coordinates the weights add to $d/16$. At $d=24$, the plane matter ground weight is $3/2$, while the strip oscillator vacuum energy is $1/2$. The plane physical condition $L_0^{\mathrm{plane}}-1=0$ is therefore equivalent to $\alpha'p_\parallel^2+N+1/2=0$. The apparent different constants are coordinate/constraint conventions, not different mass spectra.

= Closed string
{parent=Bosonic string theory}
{wiki}

A closed string has a cylindrical worldsheet without boundary. Periodicity permits independent left- and right-moving oscillator sectors and, in a compact target direction, winding modes.

= Closed-string mode expansion
{parent=Closed string}

A free closed-string embedding coordinate is the sum of center-of-mass zero modes and independent periodic left- and right-moving oscillator expansions.

= Covariant quantization of the bosonic string
{parent=Bosonic string theory}

Covariant quantization keeps target-spacetime Lorentz covariance manifest, imposes canonical commutation relations on every embedding coordinate and removes unphysical states with the <Virasoro constraints>.

= Physical string state
{parent=Covariant quantization of the bosonic string}

A physical string state obeys the Virasoro mass-shell and positive-mode constraints. States differing by a null state represent the same physical polarization.

= Spurious string state
{parent=Covariant quantization of the bosonic string}

A spurious string state is a positive-level Virasoro descendant orthogonal to all physical string states.

= Null string state
{parent=Spurious string state}

A null string state is both physical and spurious. It has zero norm and is quotiented from the physical state space.

= String tension
{title2=$T=(2\pi\alpha')^{-1}$}
{parent=Bosonic string theory}
{wiki}

String tension is energy per unit length. The Regge-slope parameter $\alpha'$ is conventionally defined by $T=(2\pi\alpha')^{-1}$.

= Regge trajectory
{title2=$J=\alpha'M^2+a$}
{c}
{parent=String tension}
{wiki}

A Regge trajectory is an approximately linear relation between angular momentum and squared mass. A classical rotating open string obeys $J=\alpha'M^2$, with quantum effects adding an intercept.

= Polyakov action
{c}
{parent=Bosonic string theory}
{wiki}

The Polyakov action introduces an independent worldsheet metric $g_{\alpha\beta}$:
$$
S_{\rm P}=-\frac T2\int_\Sigma d^2\sigma\,\sqrt{-g}\,
g^{\alpha\beta}\partial_\alpha X\mathbin\cdot\partial_\beta X.
$$
Its metric equation makes $g_{\alpha\beta}$ conformal to the <induced worldsheet metric>, reducing it classically to the <Nambu–Goto action>.

= Polyakov path integral
{c}
{parent=Polyakov action}

The Polyakov path integral integrates over string embeddings and worldsheet metrics, divided by worldsheet diffeomorphisms and Weyl transformations. Gauge fixing introduces worldsheet ghosts and moduli integrals.

= Worldsheet moduli
{parent=Polyakov path integral}
{title2=$\mathcal M_{g,n}$}

Worldsheet moduli parameterize inequivalent conformal structures and marked insertion points after <worldsheet diffeomorphisms> and <Weyl invariance> are fixed. At each <genus>, <string perturbation theory> integrates over the corresponding moduli space.

= Torus modular invariance
{parent=Worldsheet moduli}

The torus worldsheet path integral is invariant under the <modular group> acting on its complex parameter $\tau$. Integrating over the <standard fundamental domain of the modular group> counts each inequivalent torus once and excludes the short-proper-time ultraviolet region.

= Closed-string scattering amplitude
{parent=Polyakov path integral}
{title2=$\mathcal A_{g,n}$}

A closed-string <scattering amplitude> is a worldsheet correlation function of external <string vertex operators>, integrated over their unfixed positions and <worldsheet moduli>, with the necessary <worldsheet ghost fields>.

= Sphere gauge fixing for four string vertices
{parent=Closed-string scattering amplitude}

At genus zero the residual <Möbius transformation> group fixes three marked insertion points, leaving one complex position to integrate. The corresponding <worldsheet ghost fields> give $|z_{ij}z_{jk}z_{ki}|^2$ for the three fixed points. For on-shell weight-$(1,1)$ <string vertex operators>, these ghost factors cancel the coordinate dependence of the fixed-point normalization, including the limit at infinity. The remaining <Koba-Nielsen factor> for four tachyons is the integrand of the <complex beta integral>, producing the <Virasoro–Shapiro amplitude>.

= String dual resonance
{parent=Closed-string scattering amplitude}

A single <crossing-symmetric scattering amplitude> has equivalent pole expansions in different exchange channels. Its <scattering-amplitude factorization> reproduces the string spectrum and couplings in each channel without adding the channel expansions again.

= Worldsheet Green function
{parent=Polyakov path integral}

A worldsheet Green function inverts the scalar kinetic operator after its zero modes have been removed. In a locally flat complex coordinate it satisfies $-(\pi\alpha')^{-1}\partial\bar\partial G(z,w)=\delta^{(2)}(z-w)$ and is $G(z,w)=-(\alpha'/2)\log|z-w|^2$ up to harmonic and constant terms.

= String vertex operator
{parent=Polyakov path integral}
{wiki=Vertex_operator}

A string vertex operator inserts an asymptotic string state at a point of the worldsheet. Its conformal weights encode the physical-state and mass-shell conditions.

= Integrated string vertex operator
{parent=String vertex operator}

An integrated closed-string vertex is inserted as $\int d^2z\,V(z,\bar z)$. In conformal gauge its matter operator must have <conformal weights> $(1,1)$ so that the insertion is invariant under worldsheet coordinate changes.

= Massless closed-string vertex operator
{parent=Integrated string vertex operator}

At the first massless closed-string level, the matter vertex has the form $\epsilon_{\mu\nu}:\!\partial X^\mu\bar\partial X^\nu e^{ik\cdot X}\!:$, with null momentum and a polarization tensor transverse in both indices. Its symmetric trace-free, antisymmetric and trace sectors describe the graviton, <Kalb–Ramond field> and <dilaton>.

= Polarization tensor
{title2=$\epsilon_{\mu\nu}$}
{parent=Massless closed-string vertex operator}

A closed-string polarization tensor specifies the target-spacetime tensor state multiplying $\partial X^\mu\bar\partial X^\nu e^{ik\cdot X}$. Its decomposition into symmetric trace-free, antisymmetric and trace parts separates the graviton, <Kalb–Ramond field> and <dilaton> polarizations.

= String-state gauge redundancy
{parent=Massless closed-string vertex operator}

Changing a massless closed-string polarization by a momentum-longitudinal tensor changes the integrated vertex by a worldsheet total derivative, equivalently by a BRST-exact state. The corresponding target-space transformations are linearized diffeomorphisms and two-form gauge transformations.

= String mass-shell condition
{parent=String vertex operator}

= Mass-shell condition
{synonym}

A string mass-shell condition relates the target momentum of a vertex operator to its oscillator level. Together with polarization constraints, it makes the matter vertex have the conformal weights required for an integrated insertion.

= Tachyon vertex operator
{title2=$V_p={}:e^{ip\cdot X}:$}
{parent=String vertex operator}

The bosonic closed-string tachyon vertex is the normal-ordered exponential ${}:e^{ip\cdot X}:$ integrated over its worldsheet position. Pairwise contractions of such exponentials generate the <Koba-Nielsen factor>.

= Worldsheet diffeomorphism
{parent=Polyakov action}

A worldsheet diffeomorphism is a smooth reparameterization of the worldsheet coordinates under which $X^\mu$ is a scalar and $g_{\alpha\beta}$ is a rank-two tensor.

= Static gauge
{parent=Worldsheet diffeomorphism}
{wiki}

Static gauge identifies selected target-space coordinates with worldvolume coordinates, such as $X^0=\tau$ for a string.

= Monge gauge
{c}
{parent=Static gauge}

For a string, this local <static gauge> identifies two embedding coordinates with the worldsheet coordinates, $X^0=t$ and $X^1=\sigma$. The remaining coordinates describe a graph over that coordinate plane. Folded embeddings may require more than one patch.

= Weyl transformation
{c}
{parent=Polyakov action}
{wiki}

= Weyl invariance
{c}
{synonym}

A Weyl transformation rescales the worldsheet metric locally, $g_{\alpha\beta}\mapsto e^{2\omega(\sigma)}g_{\alpha\beta}$, without changing $X^\mu$. The classical Polyakov action is Weyl invariant.

= Conformal gauge
{parent=Polyakov action}
{wiki}

Conformal gauge uses worldsheet diffeomorphisms and Weyl transformations to set $g_{\alpha\beta}=e^{2\phi}\eta_{\alpha\beta}$ locally.

= Residual conformal transformation
{parent=Conformal gauge}

After conformal gauge fixing, independent reparameterizations $\sigma^+\mapsto f(\sigma^+)$ and $\sigma^-\mapsto\widetilde f(\sigma^-)$ remain, accompanied by a compensating Weyl transformation.

= Light-cone gauge in string theory
{parent=Residual conformal transformation}
{wiki=Light-cone_gauge}

Light-cone gauge uses residual conformal transformations to make the target-space coordinate $X^+$ linear in worldsheet time. The Virasoro constraints then determine $X^-$ from the transverse fields.

= Virasoro constraint
{c}
{parent=Conformal gauge}
{wiki}

The worldsheet metric equation in conformal gauge sets the stress tensor to zero, $T_{++}=T_{--}=0$. In oscillator language these are the Virasoro constraints.

= String nonlinear sigma model
{parent=Polyakov action}

The string nonlinear sigma model couples the worldsheet embedding fields to target-space backgrounds such as a metric, a <Kalb–Ramond field> and a <dilaton>. Quantum Weyl invariance requires its beta functions to vanish.

= String-frame metric
{parent=String nonlinear sigma model}
{title2=$g^{(s)}_{ab}$}

= String frame
{synonym}

The string-frame metric is the target metric appearing directly in the kinetic term of the <string nonlinear sigma model>. With conventional Euclidean <dilaton> $\varphi$, its leading spacetime gravitational action contains $\sqrt{-g^{(s)}}e^{-2\varphi}(R+4|\nabla\varphi|^2)$. The dilaton-dependent Ricci coefficient distinguishes it from the <Einstein-frame metric>. Frame changes are local field redefinitions where the conformal factor is finite and nonzero; the <Lorentzian dilaton coupling sign convention> fixes which named field is $\varphi$.

= Einstein-frame metric
{c}
{parent=String-frame metric}
{title2=$g^{(E)}_{ab}=e^{-4\varphi/(D-2)}g^{(s)}_{ab}$}

= Einstein frame
{c}
{synonym}

For $D>2$, rescale the <string-frame metric> by $g^{(E)}=e^{-4\varphi/(D-2)}g^{(s)}$. The <Weyl transformation> of the <Ricci scalar> makes the coefficient of the Einstein-frame Ricci term independent of the <dilaton>. Indeed $g^{(s)}=e^{4\varphi/(D-2)}g^{(E)}$ contributes factors $e^{2D\varphi/(D-2)}$ from the volume, $e^{-2\varphi}$ from the original coupling and $e^{-4\varphi/(D-2)}$ from the Ricci term; their product is one. Derivative terms supply the transformed dilaton kinetic term. The two-dimensional case is excluded from this particular rescaling.

= Background field expansion of a string sigma model
{parent=String nonlinear sigma model}

Expand the <string embedding map> along target geodesics as $X=\exp_{\bar X}Y$. The quadratic Euclidean fluctuation action has a <covariant derivative> term and a curvature term $-R_{acbd}Y^aY^b\partial\bar X^c\partial\bar X^d$. The logarithmically divergent coincident contraction is proportional to $\alpha'g^{ab}$; its curvature contraction is the <Ricci tensor>. A compensating metric <counterterm> gives $\beta^g_{ab}=\alpha'R_{ab}+O(\alpha'^2)$. This is why the target gravitational equation arises from quantum <worldsheet Weyl anomaly> cancellation, rather than from the classical worldsheet metric variation. Infrared regulation and removal of equation-of-motion terms by field redefinitions specify the usual one-loop scheme.

= Kalb–Ramond field
{title2=$B_{\mu\nu}$}
{c}
{parent=String nonlinear sigma model}
{wiki}

The Kalb–Ramond field is an antisymmetric two-form background coupled to the oriented worldsheet area element. Its gauge-invariant field strength is the three-form $H=dB$.

= H-field
{title2=$H=dB$}
{c}
{parent=Kalb–Ramond field}

The H-field is the three-form field strength of a <Kalb–Ramond field>. It obeys the Bianchi identity $dH=0$ and contributes quadratically to the leading string background equations.

= Sigma-model beta function
{parent=String nonlinear sigma model}

A sigma-model beta function measures the failure of a worldsheet background coupling to remain scale invariant. Vanishing metric, B-field and dilaton beta functions gives the spacetime field equations order by order in $\alpha'$.

= Leading dilaton Weyl anomaly coefficient
{parent=Sigma-model beta function}
{title2=$\overline\beta^\varphi=(D-26)/6+\alpha'[-\Box\varphi/2+|\nabla\varphi|^2]$}

For the conventional positive Euclidean <dilaton> curvature coupling, with no antisymmetric background field, the leading scalar Weyl coefficient is the displayed expression. The matter/ghost <central charge> gives $(D-26)/6$. In a geodesic <background field expansion of a string sigma model>, contracting $\tfrac12\nabla_a\nabla_b\varphi\,Y^aY^b$ in the curvature coupling produces the <counterterm> $\delta\varphi=-\alpha'\Box\varphi/(2\varepsilon)$ and hence the Laplacian term. The full embedding equation $\Box_hX^a+\Gamma^a{}_{bc}\partial X^b\partial X^c=(\alpha'/2)R^{(2)}\nabla^a\varphi$ supplies the gradient-square improvement in the dilaton Weyl variation. Setting this coefficient to zero fixes the constant in the <dilaton equation from contracted Bianchi identity>; the metric equation alone does not. The <Lorentzian dilaton coupling sign convention> determines the named-field translation.

The noncritical coefficient is also given in \a[https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/string-theory/string7.pdf][Tong's string theory notes, Section 7.4.4].

= Leading metric-dilaton Weyl condition
{parent=Sigma-model beta function}
{title2=$\overline\beta^g_{ab}=\alpha'(R_{ab}+2\nabla_a\nabla_b\varphi)$}

For the conventional Euclidean <dilaton> coupling $+(4\pi)^{-1}\int\sqrt h\,R\varphi$ and no antisymmetric background, the metric <worldsheet Weyl anomaly> vanishes at first order in $\alpha'$ when $R_{ab}+2\nabla_a\nabla_b\varphi=0$. The one-loop Ricci term follows from the <background field expansion of a string sigma model>. Under a <Weyl transformation>, the dilaton coupling varies as $-(2\pi)^{-1}\int\sqrt h\,\omega\Box_h\varphi$; the embedding equations replace this by the target Hessian contribution. Full Weyl invariance also requires the dilaton curvature coefficient, including the central-charge deficit, to vanish. See the <Lorentzian dilaton coupling sign convention> when translating an action with a specified Lorentzian sign.

The conventional coefficient agrees with the background equations in \a[https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/string-theory/string7.pdf][Tong's string theory notes, Section 7.2.3].

= Dilaton equation from contracted Bianchi identity
{parent=Leading metric-dilaton Weyl condition}
{title2=$\Box_g\varphi-2|\nabla\varphi|^2=C$}

Diverging $R_{ab}+2\nabla_a\nabla_b\varphi=0$ and using the <contracted Bianchi identity> and <Ricci identity> gives $\nabla_b(R+4\Box_g\varphi-4|\nabla\varphi|^2)=0$. The trace equation $R+2\Box_g\varphi=0$ then gives $\Box_g\varphi-2|\nabla\varphi|^2=C$, with $C$ constant on each connected component. Zero does not follow from the tensor equation alone. The additional dilaton anomaly coefficient fixes $C=(D-26)/(3\alpha')$ at leading order for the bosonic string without an antisymmetric background. In critical dimension it is zero; noncritical backgrounds have a central-charge-deficit term.

= Linear dilaton counterexample to zero integration constant
{parent=Dilaton equation from contracted Bianchi identity}

In flat target space, $\varphi=q_aX^a$ has zero Hessian and therefore solves the <leading metric-dilaton Weyl condition> for every constant covector $q$. But $\Box\varphi-2|\nabla\varphi|^2=-2q^2$, which need not vanish. This demonstrates the integration constant left by the <contracted Bianchi identity>. Full quantum Weyl invariance fixes the allowed $q^2$ through the <central charge>, as in a <linear dilaton conformal field theory>; the metric equation alone does not.

= Exponentiated dilaton wave equation
{parent=Dilaton equation from contracted Bianchi identity}
{title2=$(\Box_g+2C)e^{-2\varphi}=0$}

For the <dilaton equation from contracted Bianchi identity>, put $F=e^{-2\varphi}$. The chain rule gives $\Box_gF=F(-2\Box_g\varphi+4|\nabla\varphi|^2)=-2CF$. Thus the exponential obeys a linear <Klein-Gordon equation> on the given background, although the original <dilaton> equation is nonlinear and the <string-frame metric> remains coupled to the <dilaton>. With the opposite named-field convention $\varphi=-\Phi$, this variable is $F=e^{2\Phi}$.

= Worldsheet Weyl anomaly
{parent=Sigma-model beta function}

A worldsheet Weyl anomaly is the quantum failure of the gauge-fixed string path integral to remain invariant under a <Weyl transformation>. Its local coefficients are the <sigma-model beta functions>, while its constant part is controlled by the total <central charge>.

= Worldsheet ghost field
{title2=$b_{\alpha\beta},c^\alpha$}
{parent=Polyakov action}
{wiki=Ghosts_in_string_theory}

Gauge fixing worldsheet diffeomorphism and Weyl symmetry introduces an anticommuting vector ghost $c^\alpha$ and a symmetric traceless antighost $b_{\alpha\beta}$. In complex coordinates they form holomorphic and antiholomorphic $bc$ systems.

= BRST operator
{title2=$Q_B$}
{c}
{parent=Worldsheet ghost field}

The BRST operator is the Grassmann-odd conserved charge that combines a gauge transformation with its ghost field. Quantum gauge consistency requires $Q_B^2=0$, and physical states are its cohomology classes.

= BRST current
{title2=$j_B$}
{c}
{parent=BRST operator}

The BRST current is the conserved worldsheet current whose contour integral is the <BRST operator>. For the bosonic string it is built from the reparameterization ghost and the matter and ghost stress tensors; its self-OPE is nonsingular only when the total central charge vanishes.

= Worldsheet ghost action
{parent=Worldsheet ghost field}

For the traceless diffeomorphism operator
$$
(P_1c)_{\alpha\beta}=\nabla_\alpha c_\beta+\nabla_\beta c_\alpha-g_{\alpha\beta}\nabla_\gamma c^\gamma,
$$
the ghost action is
$$
S_{\rm gh}=\frac1{2\pi}\int d^2\sigma\,\sqrt g\,
b^{\alpha\beta}(P_1c)_{\alpha\beta}.
$$

= String worldsheet
{parent=Bosonic string theory}
{wiki=Worldsheet}

A string worldsheet $\Sigma$ is the two-dimensional surface swept out by a string in spacetime. Local coordinates are commonly denoted $\sigma^\alpha=(\tau,\sigma)$.

= Worldsheet area
{parent=String worldsheet}
{title2=$\int d^2\sigma\,\sqrt{|\det g|}$}

The area of a <string worldsheet> is computed from its <induced worldsheet metric>. The <Nambu–Goto action> is minus <string tension> times Lorentzian worldsheet area.

= String embedding map
{title2=$X^\mu(\sigma,\tau)$}
{parent=String worldsheet}

The string embedding map $X:\Sigma\to\mathcal M$ assigns each worldsheet point its position in target spacetime.

= Worldsheet zero mode
{parent=String embedding map}

A worldsheet zero mode is a field component annihilated by the worldsheet kinetic operator. For a noncompact string embedding coordinate, its constant zero mode is the target-space center-of-mass position; integrating over it enforces target-space momentum conservation.

= Induced worldsheet metric
{title2=$\gamma_{\alpha\beta}$}
{parent=String embedding map}

The induced worldsheet metric is the pullback of the target-space metric:
$$
\gamma_{\alpha\beta}=\partial_\alpha X^\mu\partial_\beta X^\nu\eta_{\mu\nu}.
$$

= Nambu–Goto action
{c}
{parent=Induced worldsheet metric}
{wiki}

The Nambu–Goto action
$$
S_{\rm NG}=-T\int_\Sigma d^2\sigma\,\sqrt{-\det\gamma}
$$
is minus the string tension times the Lorentzian area of the worldsheet.

= Rotating Nambu–Goto string
{parent=Nambu–Goto action}

A straight rigidly rotating classical string has <speed of light> at a free endpoint. Its <energy> and <angular momentum> satisfy a <Regge trajectory>. A segment with one anchored and one free end has $J=E^2/(\pi T)$, twice the slope of the string with two free ends.

= Two-dimensional conformal field theory
{title2=2D CFT}
{parent=String worldsheet}
{wiki=Two-dimensional_conformal_field_theory}

A two-dimensional conformal field theory is invariant under local holomorphic and antiholomorphic conformal transformations.

= Beta-gamma system
{title2=$\beta\gamma$ system}
{parent=Two-dimensional conformal field theory}

A beta-gamma system is a pair of commuting chiral fields with first-order action $\int\beta\bar\partial\gamma$. If $\gamma$ has weight $h$ and $\beta$ has weight $1-h$, its central charge is $2(6h^2-6h+1)$.

= bc system
{title2=$bc$ system}
{c}
{parent=Two-dimensional conformal field theory}

= bc ghost system
{c}
{synonym}

A bc system is the anticommuting counterpart of a <beta-gamma system>. For weights $h$ and $1-h$, its central charge is $-2(6h^2-6h+1)$.

= bc ghost operator-product expansion
{title2=$b(z)c(w)\sim(z-w)^{-1}$}
{c}
{parent=bc system}

For reparameterization ghosts of weights two and minus one, the canonical anticommutators imply $b(z)c(w)\sim1/(z-w)$. This operator-product expansion fixes the ghost contractions and the ghost stress tensor.

= Primary operator
{parent=Two-dimensional conformal field theory}
{wiki=Primary_field}

= Virasoro primary operator
{c}
{synonym}

A primary operator of weights $(h,\widetilde h)$ transforms covariantly under local conformal maps and has a universal operator-product expansion with the stress-energy tensor.

= Conformal weight
{title2=$(h,\widetilde h)$}
{parent=Primary operator}
{wiki}

The conformal weights determine the response of a primary operator to holomorphic and antiholomorphic coordinate rescaling. Its scaling dimension is $\Delta=h+\widetilde h$ and its spin is $h-\widetilde h$.

= Virasoro descendant
{c}
{parent=Primary operator}

A Virasoro descendant is obtained by applying negative Virasoro modes $L_{-n}$ to a primary state or operator. Its level is the sum of the mode numbers.

= Operator product expansion
{title2=OPE}
{parent=Two-dimensional conformal field theory}
{wiki}

An operator product expansion expresses the short-distance product of local operators as a sum of local operators multiplied by singular coefficient functions.

= Holomorphic stress-energy tensor
{title2=$T(z)$}
{parent=Two-dimensional conformal field theory}

The holomorphic stress-energy tensor generates infinitesimal holomorphic conformal transformations through its operator product expansions.

= Stress-tensor operator-product expansion
{title2=$T(z)T(w)$ OPE}
{parent=Holomorphic stress-energy tensor}

In a two-dimensional conformal field theory,
$$
T(z)T(w)\sim\frac{c/2}{(z-w)^4}+\frac{2T(w)}{(z-w)^2}+\frac{\partial T(w)}{z-w}.
$$
The fourth-order pole is the central extension, while the remaining poles express that $T$ has conformal weight two.

= Virasoro algebra
{title2=$[L_m,L_n]=(m-n)L_{m+n}+\frac{c}{12}m(m^2-1)\delta_{m+n,0}$}
{c}
{parent=Holomorphic stress-energy tensor}
{wiki}

The Virasoro algebra is the central extension of the algebra of holomorphic vector fields on a circle. Its generators are the Laurent modes of the holomorphic stress-energy tensor, and its central term is controlled by the <central charge> $c$.

= Virasoro central extension
{c}
{parent=Virasoro algebra}

The Virasoro central extension is the term $c(m^3-m)\delta_{m+n,0}/12$ in $[L_m,L_n]$. It records the conformal anomaly and commutes with every Virasoro generator.

= Central charge
{title2=$c$}
{parent=Holomorphic stress-energy tensor}
{wiki}

The central charge is the coefficient of the fourth-order pole in the stress-tensor operator-product expansion, $T(z)T(w)\sim c/[2(z-w)^4]+\cdots$. A free real boson contributes one and a free real chiral fermion contributes one half.

= Superconformal current
{title2=$G(z)$}
{parent=Central charge}

In a two-dimensional supersymmetric conformal field theory, the superconformal current has weight $3/2$ and its self-OPE closes on the stress tensor and the <central charge>.

= N=1 super-Virasoro algebra
{title2=$\mathcal N=1$ super-Virasoro algebra}
{c}
{parent=Superconformal current}

The holomorphic N=1 super-Virasoro algebra extends the Virasoro algebra by fermionic modes $G_r$ satisfying $\{G_r,G_s\}=2L_{r+s}+(c/12)(4r^2-1)\delta_{r,-s}$.

= Free boson conformal field theory
{parent=Two-dimensional conformal field theory}

The free boson conformal field theory has action quadratic in a scalar $X$ and central charge one. Its oscillator partition function is expressed through the <Dedekind eta function>.

= Free-boson worldsheet propagator
{parent=Free boson conformal field theory}

For the normalization $S=(2\pi\alpha')^{-1}\int d^2z\,\partial X\bar\partial X$, the free-boson worldsheet propagator has singular part
$$
X^\mu(z,\bar z)X^\nu(w,\bar w)\sim-\frac{\alpha'}2\eta^{\mu\nu}\log|z-w|^2.
$$
Differentiation gives the singular operator-product expansions of the embedding currents.

= Linear dilaton conformal field theory
{parent=Free boson conformal field theory}

A linear dilaton conformal field theory improves the free-boson stress tensor by a second derivative, $T=-\alpha'^{-1}:\!\partial X\partial X\!:-q\partial^2X$. The improvement shifts the <central charge> to $c=1+6\alpha'q^2$ and corresponds to a dilaton linear in the target coordinate $X$.

= Background-charge scalar field
{parent=Linear dilaton conformal field theory}

A background-charge scalar field is a free scalar coupled linearly to the worldsheet Ricci scalar. This curvature coupling produces the improved stress tensor of a <linear dilaton conformal field theory>.

= Torus partition function of a free boson
{parent=Free boson conformal field theory}

For a noncompact free boson with target volume $V$, the torus partition function is $Z=V[2\pi\sqrt{\operatorname{Im}\tau}]^{-1}|\eta(\tau)|^{-2}$ in the normalization where the zero-mode Hamiltonian is $p^2/2$.

= Dedekind eta function
{title2=$\eta(\tau)$}
{c}
{parent=Torus partition function of a free boson}
{wiki}

The Dedekind eta function is
$$
\eta(\tau)=q^{1/24}\prod_{n=1}^{\infty}(1-q^n),
\qquad q=e^{2\pi i\tau}.
$$
It packages the oscillator contribution of a chiral free boson.

= Compact boson
{parent=Free boson conformal field theory}
{wiki}

A compact boson takes values on a circle, $X\sim X+2\pi R$. Its states carry quantized momentum $n/R$ and integer winding $wR$.

= Momentum and winding modes
{title2=$(n,w)$}
{parent=Compact boson}

For a compact boson of radius $R$, momentum number $n$ and winding number $w$ combine into left- and right-moving zero-mode momenta $p_L=n/R+wR$ and $p_R=n/R-wR$ in a common normalization.

= T-duality
{c}
{parent=Momentum and winding modes}
{wiki}

T-duality identifies a compact boson of radius $R$ with one of reciprocal radius by exchanging momentum and winding numbers. Factors of $\alpha'$ restore the dimensional relation $R\leftrightarrow\alpha'/R$.

= Buscher procedure
{c}
{parent=T-duality}

The Buscher procedure derives T-dual backgrounds by replacing the derivative of an isometric target coordinate with an independent worldsheet one-form and imposing its flatness with a Lagrange multiplier. Integrating out either field yields the original or dual sigma model.

= Buscher rules
{c}
{parent=Buscher procedure}

The Buscher rules invert the metric component along an Abelian isometry and transform the other metric and B-field components. The Gaussian functional determinant also shifts the dilaton; when the metric is block diagonal with $G_{zz}=V$, $\widetilde\Phi=\Phi-\tfrac12\log V$.

= String oscillator
{parent=Bosonic string theory}

The Fourier modes $\alpha_n^i$ and $\widetilde\alpha_n^i$ of the transverse closed-string coordinates obey harmonic-oscillator commutation relations. Modes with negative index create string excitations.

= Half-integer open-string oscillator
{parent=String oscillator}
{title2=$\alpha_r^i,\quad r\in\mathbb Z+\tfrac12$}

For a <Neumann-Dirichlet open-string boundary condition>, the transverse modes have frequencies $r\in\mathbb Z+1/2$. The <canonical commutation relations> give $[\alpha_r^i,\alpha_s^j]=r\delta^{ij}\delta_{r+s,0}$ and $\alpha_r^\dagger=\alpha_{-r}$. One derivation expands $X-y=\sum_{r>0}q_r\cos(r\sigma)$: spatial <orthogonality> reduces the <Polyakov action> to $(8\alpha')^{-1}\int\sum_r(\dot q_r^2-r^2q_r^2)d\tau$. These are <harmonic oscillators> of <mass> $1/(4\alpha')$, whose normalized <creation operators> satisfy $\alpha_{-r}=i\sqrt r\,a_r^\dagger$ in the conventional phased expansion.

= String level operator
{title2=$N$}
{parent=String oscillator}

The string level operator is the weighted oscillator number $N=\sum_{n>0}\alpha_{-n}\mathbin\cdot\alpha_n$. A closed string has independent operators $N$ and $\widetilde N$ for its two chiral sectors.

= Closed-string level matching
{parent=String oscillator}

Closed-string periodicity and the residual spatial constraint require equal left- and right-moving excitation levels, $N=\widetilde N$.

= Normal-ordering constant of a string
{title2=$a$}
{parent=String oscillator}

The normal-ordering constant is the zero-point shift in the quantum Virasoro generator. Closure of the target-space Lorentz algebra fixes $a=1$ and the spacetime dimension $D=26$ for the bosonic string.

= Neumann-Dirichlet string zero-point energy
{c}
{parent=Normal-ordering constant of a string}
{title2=$E_0=d/48$}

Each of $d$ physical transverse <bosons> with a <Neumann-Dirichlet open-string boundary condition> contributes $\tfrac12\sum_{r>0}r=1/48$ by the <half-integer zeta-regularized mode sum>. Thus $L_0^\perp=N+d/48$ and the convention $L_0=\alpha'p^2+N-a$ has $a=-d/48$. For $d=24$, the vacuum shift is $+1/2$. The ordinary integer-moded open-string shift is instead $-d/24$; these endpoint sectors have different normal-ordering constants.

= Lowest levels of a fully transverse ND bosonic string
{parent=Neumann-Dirichlet string zero-point energy}

With 24 physical <half-integer open-string oscillators>, the first three <string level operator> <eigenvalues> are $0,1/2,1$. Their states are the vacuum, $a_{1/2}^{i\dagger}|0\rangle$, and $a_{1/2}^{i\dagger}a_{1/2}^{j\dagger}|0\rangle$. The <creation operators> commute, so the third level is the <symmetric square> of the 24-dimensional transverse vector, of dimension $24\cdot25/2=300$. Its trace is a scalar and its trace-free part has dimension 299. Including the vacuum shift gives $\alpha'M^2=1/2,1,3/2$ when the two longitudinal directions are common directions with <Neumann boundary conditions>. The representation labels refer to the surviving ND rotation group, not full unbroken target Lorentz symmetry.

= Bosonic string mass spectrum
{parent=String oscillator}

For a closed bosonic string,
$$
M^2=\frac4{\alpha'}(N-a)=\frac4{\alpha'}(\widetilde N-a),
\qquad N=\widetilde N.
$$

= Critical dimension of string theory
{parent=Bosonic string theory}
{wiki=Critical_dimension}

The critical dimension is the target-spacetime dimension in which quantum anomalies obstructing Lorentz or Weyl symmetry cancel. It is 26 for the bosonic string.

= Critical dimension of the RNS superstring
{parent=Critical dimension of string theory}
{title2=$D=10$}

Each of $D$ embedding <bosons> contributes one to the chiral <central charge>, and each real <worldsheet Majorana fermion> contributes one half. The reparameterization <bc system> contributes $-26$, and the <superconformal ghosts> contribute $+11$. Vanishing total <worldsheet Weyl anomaly> therefore requires $3D/2-26+11=0$, or $D=10$. This differs from the critical dimension 26 of the bosonic string, which has no worldsheet <fermions> or superconformal ghosts.

= Koba-Nielsen factor
{c}
{parent=Bosonic string theory}
{wiki=Koba–Nielsen_formula}

The Koba-Nielsen factor is the product of powers of pairwise worldsheet insertion-point distances produced by contractions of string vertex operators.

= Virasoro–Shapiro amplitude
{c}
{parent=Koba-Nielsen factor}
{wiki}

The Virasoro–Shapiro amplitude is the crossing-symmetric tree-level four-point amplitude of closed-string tachyons. Its worldsheet integral is a complex beta integral, and its poles display the closed-string spectrum.

= Virasoro–Shapiro amplitude pole residue
{c}
{parent=Virasoro–Shapiro amplitude}
{title2=$s_n=4(n-1)/\alpha'$}

For $a=-1-\alpha's/4$, $b=-1-\alpha't/4$ and $a+b+c=1$, the amplitude has generic <simple poles> at $a=-n$. The <gamma function> <residue> and recurrence give
$$
\operatorname*{Res}_{s=s_n}A^{(4)}=-\frac{8\pi g_s^2}{\alpha'(n!)^2}\prod_{j=1}^n(b-j)^2.
$$
The <residue> polynomial has degree $2n$, consistent with closed-string exchange up to spin $2n$. When $a=-n+\delta_a$ and $b=-m+\delta_b$, the reciprocal factor $1/\Gamma(a+b)$ removes a putative double pole: the leading ratio is $\binom{n+m}{n}^2(1/\delta_a+1/\delta_b)$. Thus channel singularities must be counted using the kinematic constraint, not by multiplying independent numerator poles.

= CHY formalism
{c}
{parent=Koba-Nielsen factor}
{wiki}

The Cachazo-He-Yuan formalism expresses tree-level scattering amplitudes as integrals localized on scattering equations.

= Scattering equation
{parent=CHY formalism}

For null or hard external momenta, the scattering equations are
$$
\sum_{j\ne i}\frac{p_i\cdot p_j}{z_i-z_j}=0.
$$
They arise as stationary-point equations for the Koba-Nielsen exponent and are covariant under projective transformations of the insertion points.

= Type II superstring theory
{c}
{parent=String theory}
{wiki=Type_II_string_theory}

Type II superstring theory is a ten-dimensional closed oriented superstring theory with spacetime supersymmetry and no tachyon. Its massive closed-string levels have squared masses separated by $4/\alpha'$.

= Type II superstring mass spectrum
{c}
{parent=Type II superstring theory}

The four-point tree amplitude exposes poles at $M_n^2=4n/\alpha'$ for $n=0,1,2,\ldots$. The $n=0$ level is massless, and positive $n$ form an infinite tower of massive string excitations.

= D-brane
{c}
{parent=String theory}
{wiki}

A D-brane is a hypersurface on which open-string endpoints obey Neumann boundary conditions tangentially and Dirichlet boundary conditions transversely. Its massless open-string vector mode is a worldvolume gauge field.

= Open string stretched between D-branes
{c}
{parent=D-brane}

An open string stretched a distance $L$ between parallel D-branes has squared mass $M^2=T^2L^2+(N-1)/\alpha'$. The stretching energy makes strings between separated branes massive.

= Coincident-D-brane gauge enhancement
{c}
{parent=Open string stretched between D-branes}

When $n$ parallel D-branes coincide, oriented strings joining distinct branes become massless alongside strings whose ends lie on one brane. Their Chan-Paton labels fill the adjoint representation of $U(n)$, enhancing $U(1)^n$ to $U(n)$.

= Open-string boundary condition in a B-field
{parent=D-brane}

For a D-brane in metric $G$, pulled-back B-field $B$ and worldvolume field strength $F$, tangential open-string coordinates obey the mixed condition $G_{ab}\partial_\sigma X^b+(B+2\pi\alpha'F)_{ab}\partial_\tau X^b=0$. Only the gauge-invariant combination $B+2\pi\alpha'F$ is physical.

= Gauge-gravity duality
{parent=String theory}
{wiki}

Gauge/gravity duality relates a quantum field theory without dynamical gravity to a gravitational or string theory in one additional emergent dimension.

= Conformal field theory
{title2=CFT}
{parent=Gauge-gravity duality}
{wiki}

A conformal field theory is a quantum field theory invariant under conformal transformations. Local operators are organized into conformal families labelled by scaling dimension and spin.

= Conformal cross-ratio
{parent=Conformal field theory}

A conformal cross-ratio is a dimensionless combination of point separations invariant under conformal transformations. Four generic points in dimension greater than two have two independent cross-ratios,
$$
u=\frac{x_{12}^2x_{34}^2}{x_{13}^2x_{24}^2},
\qquad
v=\frac{x_{14}^2x_{23}^2}{x_{13}^2x_{24}^2}.
$$

= Scaling dimension
{title2=$\Delta$}
{parent=Conformal field theory}
{wiki}

The scaling dimension of a local operator is its eigenvalue under dilations. Under $x\mapsto\lambda x$, a scalar operator of dimension $\Delta$ transforms by $\mathcal O(x)\mapsto\lambda^{-\Delta}\mathcal O(\lambda x)$.

= Exactly marginal operator
{parent=Scaling dimension}

An exactly marginal operator has scaling dimension equal to the spacetime dimension and a deformation coupling whose beta function vanishes to all orders. Varying that coupling moves along a family of conformal field theories without introducing a scale.

= Conformal algebra
{parent=Conformal field theory}
{wiki}

The conformal algebra in $d$ Euclidean dimensions is generated by translations $P_a$, rotations $M_{ab}$, dilations $D$, and special conformal transformations $K_a$.

= Conformal primary operator
{parent=Conformal field theory}
{wiki}

A conformal primary operator is annihilated at the origin by every special conformal generator. Acting with translation generators produces its conformal descendants.

= Scalar primary operator
{parent=Conformal primary operator}

A scalar primary operator transforms without a spin rotation and is characterized by its <scaling dimension>. Conformal symmetry fixes its two- and three-point functions up to constants, while a four-point function can depend on conformal cross-ratios.

= Conformal descendant
{parent=Conformal primary operator}
{wiki}

A conformal descendant is obtained by applying one or more translation generators to a <conformal primary operator>. Its scaling dimension exceeds that of the primary by a nonnegative integer.

= Conformal unitarity bound
{parent=Conformal primary operator}
{wiki}

Positivity of descendant norms constrains the scaling dimension of a primary operator. For a nonidentity scalar primary in $d\geq2$, unitarity requires $\Delta\geq(d-2)/2$.

= State–operator correspondence
{parent=Conformal field theory}
{wiki}

The state–operator correspondence maps a local operator of scaling dimension $\Delta$ to a state on $S^{d-1}\times\mathbb R$ whose cylinder energy is $\Delta/r$ for sphere radius $r$.

= Low-energy pure-gravity spectrum in AdS4
{title2=Low-energy pure-gravity spectrum in $\mathrm{AdS}_4$}
{c}
{parent=State–operator correspondence}

If the only light field in AdS4 is the graviton, the dual CFT3 spectrum begins with the vacuum at dimension zero, the spin-two stress tensor at dimension three, and its non-null level-one descendants of spins two and three at dimension four.

= AdS-CFT correspondence
{c}
{parent=Gauge-gravity duality}
{wiki=AdS/CFT_correspondence}

The AdS/CFT correspondence identifies a conformal field theory with quantum gravity on an asymptotically anti-de Sitter spacetime. Boundary sources couple to CFT operators and equal boundary values of bulk fields.

= Euclidean conformal transformation
{parent=AdS-CFT correspondence}

A Euclidean conformal transformation pulls the flat metric back to a position-dependent positive multiple of itself. Its infinitesimal generator obeys the conformal Killing equation.

= Scalar-primary two-point function
{parent=AdS-CFT correspondence}

Conformal invariance fixes the two-point function of identical scalar primaries of dimension $\Delta$ to $C_2/|x_{12}|^{2\Delta}$.

= Scalar-primary three-point function
{parent=AdS-CFT correspondence}

Three scalar primaries have a conformally fixed position dependence, leaving one theory-dependent coefficient $C_{123}$.

= Witten diagram
{c}
{parent=AdS-CFT correspondence}
{wiki}

A Witten diagram is a position-space perturbative diagram in anti-de Sitter spacetime whose external bulk-to-boundary propagators end at boundary operator insertions.

= Single-trace operator
{parent=AdS-CFT correspondence}
{wiki}

In a large-rank gauge theory, a single-trace operator is dual at leading order to a single bulk field, while multi-trace operators correspond to multiparticle bulk states.

= Holographic dictionary
{parent=AdS-CFT correspondence}
{wiki}

The holographic dictionary identifies leading near-boundary bulk-field coefficients with CFT sources and subleading normalizable coefficients with operator expectation values.

= Fefferman--Graham coordinates
{c}
{parent=Holographic dictionary}
{wiki}

Fefferman--Graham coordinates put an asymptotically anti-de Sitter metric in the form $ds^2=L^2[dZ^2/Z^2+g_{ij}(Z,x)dx^idx^j/Z^2]$.

= AdS scalar-field boundary asymptotics
{parent=Holographic dictionary}

A scalar field of mass $m$ in $\operatorname{AdS}_{d+1}$ behaves near the conformal boundary as $\phi=z^{d-\Delta}J+z^\Delta A+\cdots$, where $m^2=\Delta(\Delta-d)$. The leading coefficient is the source and the normalizable coefficient determines the operator expectation value.

= AdS scalar mass--dimension relation
{title2=$m^2L^2=\Delta(\Delta-d)$}
{c}
{parent=AdS scalar-field boundary asymptotics}

For a scalar field of mass $m$ on $\operatorname{AdS}_{d+1}$ of radius $L$, the scaling dimension of the dual scalar primary obeys $m^2L^2=\Delta(\Delta-d)$. The two roots correspond to the two independent near-boundary falloffs.

= Alternative quantization of an AdS scalar field
{title2=Alternative quantization of an AdS scalar field}
{c}
{parent=AdS scalar-field boundary asymptotics}

When both scalar falloffs in anti-de Sitter spacetime are normalizable, alternative quantization exchanges which near-boundary coefficient is treated as the source and which as the expectation value. In AdS4, a field with $m^2=-2$ may be dual to a scalar operator of dimension one.

= Holographic renormalization
{parent=Holographic dictionary}
{wiki}

Holographic renormalization subtracts local boundary counterterms from the regulated on-shell bulk action. It gives finite correlation functions and identifies the renormalized normalizable coefficient with an operator expectation value.

= Holographic entanglement entropy
{parent=Holographic dictionary}
{wiki}

At leading semiclassical order, holographic entanglement entropy is the area of a bulk extremal surface anchored on the boundary of the chosen region, divided by $4G_N$.

= Ryu–Takayanagi formula
{c}
{parent=Holographic entanglement entropy}
{wiki}

For a static holographic state and boundary region $A$,
$$
S(A)=\frac{\operatorname{Area}(\gamma_A)}{4G_N},
$$
where $\gamma_A$ is the least-area bulk surface homologous to $A$ and anchored on $\partial A$.

= Ryu–Takayanagi surface for a disk
{c}
{parent=Ryu–Takayanagi formula}

For a round disk of radius $R$ in the vacuum of a holographic conformal field theory on flat space, the static Poincare-AdS Ryu--Takayanagi surface is the hemisphere $r^2+z^2=R^2$.

= Brown--Henneaux central charge
{title2=$c=3R_{\rm AdS}/(2G)$}
{c}
{parent=Holographic entanglement entropy}

The Brown--Henneaux central charge is the central charge of the two-dimensional conformal field theory dual to Einstein gravity on $\operatorname{AdS}_3$:
$$
c=\frac{3R_{\rm AdS}}{2G_3}.
$$

= Casimir energy of a two-dimensional conformal field theory
{title2=$E_0=-c/12$}
{parent=Brown--Henneaux central charge}

For a unit spatial circle, the vacuum energy of a two-dimensional conformal field theory is $E_0=-c/12$. It arises from the anomalous Schwarzian shift when the plane vacuum is mapped to the cylinder and is dual to the renormalized energy of global $\operatorname{AdS}_3$.

= Hubeny–Rangamani–Takayanagi surface
{title2=HRT surface}
{c}
{parent=Holographic entanglement entropy}

A Hubeny–Rangamani–Takayanagi surface is the covariant extremal-area surface homologous to a boundary region. Its area divided by $4G_N$ gives leading semiclassical entanglement entropy in a time-dependent holographic spacetime.

= One-sided unitary excitation of a thermofield double state
{parent=Hubeny–Rangamani–Takayanagi surface}

A unitary acting on only one factor of a thermofield double state preserves the spectrum of either reduced density matrix and hence preserves left--right entanglement. A bulk shell created on one boundary can enlarge the late event horizon while the HRT surface remains at the earlier bifurcation surface.

= Thermofield double state
{title2=TFD state}
{parent=Holographic entanglement entropy}
{wiki}

For two copies of a quantum system,
$$
|\Psi_\beta\rangle=Z(\beta)^{-1/2}
\sum_ne^{-\beta E_n/2}|n\rangle_L^*|n\rangle_R.
$$
Tracing out either copy produces the thermal density operator $e^{-\beta H}/Z$.

= Modular Hamiltonian
{title2=$K=-\log\rho$}
{parent=Thermofield double state}
{wiki}

The modular Hamiltonian of a density operator is $K=-\log\rho$. For a Gibbs state, $K=\beta H+\log Z$.

= First law of entanglement entropy
{parent=Modular Hamiltonian}

Linearizing the von Neumann entropy around a reference state gives $\delta S=\operatorname{Tr}(\delta\rho K)$. Around a thermal state this becomes $\delta S=\beta\,\delta\langle H\rangle$.

= Holographic mutual information
{parent=Holographic entanglement entropy}

Holographic mutual information is nonnegative because the disconnected union $\gamma_A\cup\gamma_B$ is an admissible surface for $A\cup B$, whose minimizing surface can have no larger area.

= Entanglement-wedge phase transition
{parent=Holographic mutual information}

As two boundary regions separate, the minimizing surface for their union can switch from connected to disconnected. At leading order this produces a continuous entropy with a kink and makes holographic mutual information vanish beyond a critical separation.

= Holographic chemical potential
{parent=Holographic dictionary}

For a bulk gauge field, the leading boundary value of its time component is the dual chemical potential, while the normalizable radial coefficient determines the charge density.

= Holographic D.C. conductivity
{parent=Holographic dictionary}

At nonzero charge density, a translationally invariant holographic state has infinite D.C. conductivity because an electric field continuously injects conserved momentum. Momentum relaxation broadens the zero-frequency delta function and makes the D.C. value finite.

= ABJM theory
{c}
{parent=AdS-CFT correspondence}
{wiki}

ABJM theory is a three-dimensional supersymmetric Chern–Simons matter theory with a large-$N$ holographic dual. In its M-theory regime the bulk Newton constant scales as $G_N\sim N^{-3/2}$ in AdS units.
